xecution seam for CLI reviewer sessions.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"c12-budget-functions","ref":"assumptions / k5","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"createReviewBudgetPolicy and measureReviewBudget, both in src/workflow/review-pipeline/budget.ts:86-149, provide the existing estimated inclusive budgeting that the epic's continuation policy must integrate with.","evidence":"read src/workflow/review-pipeline/budget.ts:86-149 confirms both cited symbols exist exactly where claimed: createReviewBudgetPolicy begins the range (grep confirms its definition sits at the start of this span, immediately followed by validateReviewBudgetPolicy), and measureReviewBudget (grep: defined at budget.ts:110) runs through the end of the cited range — admitReviewBudget, which calls measureReviewBudget at line 151, begins at line 150, i.e. immediately after the cited 86-149 span. The read body shows measureReviewBudget computing an estimatedTotalTokens that sums promptTokens, schemaTokens, declaredToolsTokens, nativeReserveTokens, transportReserveTokens, completionReserveTokens, inspectionReserveTokens, hostContextReserveTokens and retryHeadroomTokens — i.e. an inclusive, estimated budget exactly as the premise describes — and createReviewBudgetPolicy builds the ReviewBudgetPolicy consumed by it. Widespread call sites (read-only-tool-loop.ts, review-budget.ts, measurement.ts, multiple test files) corroborate these are the live, existing budgeting primitives.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"c9-modelDimensionAdapter","ref":"references / s1,s3 Extends","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"modelDimensionAdapter in src/workflow/review-pipeline/code-review.ts:159-220 prepares material and configures source inspection, chunk calls and gather for a review dimension, and is the existing mechanism that stories s1 and s3 extend.","evidence":"The read of src/workflow/review-pipeline/code-review.ts:159-220 confirms the premise verbatim: `export function modelDimensionAdapter<O>(spec, subject, snapshot, limitChars, path, budgetPolicy): ReviewAdapter<DimensionResult>` builds `execution` (source-inspection vs structured mode), a lazily-built `material()`, a `stages()` function that assembles model/deterministic stages with `chunkCalls`, and returns an object that conditionally includes `gather: codeReviewGather(...)` — i.e. it prepares material and configures source inspection, chunk calls and gather for a review dimension, exactly as cited. The comments at this exact site already read 'Story s3: its obligations and their context' and 'Story s3: a divided review judges each obligation once, over every chunk's notes', independently corroborating s3 as an extension point of this mechanism. 's1' does not appear in these lines, but that is expected if s1 is a new, not-yet-built story proposing to extend this adapter — the gathered grep/read evidence cannot contradict a forward-looking design claim about an unbuilt story.","action":"none — verified sound; the function, its location, and its described behavior match the real source exactly, and the s3 extension role is independently corroborated by existing in-code comments.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"c4-subject-upstream-contract","ref":"k6 / references","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/code-review/subject.ts:121-177 is the existing implementation backing constraint k6's requirement that builds without a PLAN use their approved upstream contract and report the basis rather than inventing tasks.","evidence":"The read of src/workflow/code-review/subject.ts:121-177 shows resolveCodeReviewSubject resolving approvedLld and approvedPlan independently, each in its OWN try/catch: ArtifactMissingError or ArtifactNotApprovedError for either is caught and mapped to null for that field only, while any other error propagates unchanged. This is exactly the 'use their approved upstream contract' mechanism k6 describes — when no approved PLAN exists, approvedPlan is simply null and approvedLld (the upstream contract) remains available, with both fields carried forward (not shown past line 177, but set up here) rather than any fabricated/invented task list being constructed in this block.","action":"none — verified sound; the cited lines correctly implement the optional, independently-resolved LLD/PLAN contract fallback that k6 requires. If the reviewer wants the 'report the basis' half of k6 pinned down too, extend the citation a little past line 177 to the return/result construction, but that is a refinement, not a correction.","fixability":"manual"},{"claimId":"c5-prior-epic-compat-boundary","ref":"k8 / references","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"The prior epic artifact docs/epics/chunked-review-pipeline-every-review-E202610198315a11f/DEF.md, lines 27-34, establishes the compatibility boundary that constraint k8 requires this epic's successor mechanism to respect when superseding predecessor code-review chunking.","evidence":"Reading docs/epics/chunked-review-pipeline-every-review-E202610198315a11f/DEF.md lines 27-34 confirms the cited lines contain FR001-FR008 of the prior epic: FR001 (every review passes through the same prepare/review/gather stages), FR003-FR006 (how chunking divides material, carries cross-chunk summaries, guarantees nothing is cut or dropped, and gathers chunk findings into one equivalent-to-whole-material result), FR007 (per-call unique identifiers to prevent duplicate sends), and FR008 (the chunk limit is one token-based setting, default 128K). This is exactly the functional contract (chunk boundaries, dedup/no-drop guarantees, gather-stage equivalence, and the single tunable limit) that a successor mechanism superseding this chunking approach must remain compatible with. The anchor resolves to the correct file and line range and the content is on-topic for the stated constraint.","action":"none - verified sound","fixabi